It does matter, and the remainder of your paragraph was ad hominem
vitriol rather than a reasonable discussion.  The quoted dispute is,
in fact, enormously important in this discussion.  It is probably the
single most important reason that Klaus' numbers are way off--as I've
posted earlier, it's probably responsible for at least a (base-2)
order of magnitude error in his numbers (that is, I believe that more
than half of the  *healing potions _that I actually recover and get
into inventory_  are from defensive item generation or other sources,
rather than the random chances given in the spoiler).

I've been a huge defender of yours in the past, and I hope that this
thread is just a blip on the screen.  The fact is that Derek has
raised very important points in this thread, including that the number
of potions generated by monsters as defense items vastly outstrips the
number randomly generated on the floor, and that Klaus has
persistently failed in his analysis (e.g by neglecting to realize that
potion generation rates vary by dungeon level, that percentage of
healing potions likewise varies by dungeon level, and that player play
style has such a  severe impact on generation rates that attempting to
extrapolate from personal experience isn't very useful, and also by
insisting on order-of-magnitude calculations that are reasonable in
certain circumstances but far off base in this discussion).

You have persisted in missing the forest for the trees and attacking
Derek personally rather than realizing that his criticisms of the
probabilities are likely quite apt.  I think you're usually pretty
good at contributing to conversations, and I hope that you take this
as constructive criticism rather than anything personal--you (and
pretty much everyone) are much more interesting to read when you
consider and discuss the thread without bringing past personal
conflicts into the conversation.

